{%- comment -%}
  PG Wall Art gallery.

  The same two changes pg-gallery-tweaks makes on the crystal orbs page,
  applied to the wall art page:

  1. The room shot that used to stand on top of the gallery was dropped at the
     owner's request. Hiding .pgx-hero is what lifts the lenticular flip video
     into that slot, because the video is the next child in pg-landing's DOM.
  2. Below it, pg-landing lays the photo strip out two-up on desktop. That
     sizing was for thumbnails sitting UNDER a big hero; with the hero gone the
     strip IS the gallery, so a pair of small tiles is too little to be the only
     picture of the product. Desktop now shows one photo per view, paged by the
     arrows pg-landing already draws at that breakpoint.

  Under 900px the strip is already one photo per view: pg-gallery-mobile does
  that site-wide from footer-group. This file only has to handle desktop.

  The hero is HIDDEN, not deleted, and no product media was touched. That photo
  is still the product's featured image, so it goes on carrying the collection
  card, the bundle tile and the cart line. The strip's buttons still set the
  hidden hero's src; they simply set it on something nobody sees.

  3. The single gallery video is now a ten-slide video slideshow, one clip per
     variant, and selecting a design anywhere on the page -- the buy-box
     dropdowns or the variant picker inside the cart drawer -- moves the
     slideshow to that design's clip. Arrows and dots page through it by hand.
     The nine newer clips play at 2.5x, the original at normal speed; the rate
     is per clip, as `r` in the script.

  WHY THE SLIDESHOW IS BUILT IN JS RATHER THAN IN pg-landing. That section is
  shared by the homepage and five product templates and renders ONE video from
  its `gallery_video_url` setting. Teaching it about a per-variant video list
  would push wall-art-only markup into every page that uses it. This file is
  scoped to this template, so the whole feature lives here and pg-landing is
  untouched. If the JS never runs, pg-landing's original single <video> is what
  stays on screen -- the takeover is the enhancement, not the baseline.

  TWO VIDEO ELEMENTS, NOT TEN. Ten <video> tags would have the browser opening
  ten connections for clips nobody asked for. Swapping `src` on ONE element
  blanks it to black the instant the new source is set, which is a visible flash
  on every variant change. So there are two stacked elements and the incoming
  clip is loaded off-screen, faded in only once it has frames to show, and the
  outgoing one is paused after the fade. Nothing but the first clip is fetched
  until a shopper actually asks for it.

  ASPECT. Every slide is the same size, 990x1080 -- the shape of the original
  gallery clip, which is what set the height of this slot before there was a
  slideshow. The box never changes, so paging or switching variant never moves
  the page.

  The nine newer clips do not natively fit that box. They are 1920x1080 FILES
  with roughly 9:16 footage pillarboxed inside them; the black bars are baked
  into the frames, not letterboxing added by the layout. Three attempts, and
  only the third holds:

    - CONTAIN in a 16:9 box. Reads the files as genuine 16:9 content and fits
      the whole frame in, so every baked-in bar stays on screen and the footage
      shrinks to make room for them.
    - COVER in the 990x1080 box. Cover only crops what OVERFLOWS. Tall narrow
      footage scaled into a wider box does not overflow it sideways, it falls
      SHORT -- measured on the page, the footage reached about 64% of the box
      width and the remaining 36% stayed black. Cover cannot remove a bar the
      box is too wide to hide.
    - ZOOM, which is this. `z` on each entry scales the element up until the
      footage itself is wider than the box, so the bars are pushed off both
      edges and clipped away.

  WHERE 1.66 COMES FROM. Cover already fits the file's height to the box, so the
  footage lands at (footage aspect / box aspect) of the box width. For 9:16 in
  990x1080 that is 0.5625 / 0.9167 = 0.61, and the page measured about 0.64;
  1 / 0.61 = 1.63 is the theoretical fill and 1 / 0.64 = 1.56 the measured one.
  1.66 clears both with a few percent to spare, because a bar left showing is
  the visible failure here and a few extra cropped pixels are not.

  WHAT IT COSTS. Zooming past the width crops the height: about 40% of these
  clips, a fifth off the top and a fifth off the bottom. That is inherent -- 9:16
  footage cannot fill a 0.9167 box any other way. The clips are framed with the
  poster centred, so the crop takes room rather than subject, but it IS a crop.
  Re-encoding the nine to 990x1080 with the footage centred would let `z` go
  back to 1 and cost nothing at all.

  Rendered only from templates/product.pg-wallart.json, so removing it from
  that template's order puts the original gallery back in one edit.
{%- endcomment -%}
<style>
  /* the room shot -- see the note above */
  #pgx .pgx-hero{display:none !important}
  /* the video leads the column now, so it does not need the gap that used to
     separate it from the hero */
  #pgx .pgx-vid{margin-top:0 !important}

  /* the strip is the gallery now, not a row of thumbnails under one */
  @media (min-width:901px){
    #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pgx-thumbs button{flex:0 0 100% !important}
  }

  /* ---- the video slideshow ---- */
  /* one box for all ten slides: the original clip's shape. overflow:hidden is
     load-bearing, not tidiness -- it is what clips the zoomed-off bars. */
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-stage{position:relative;width:100%;aspect-ratio:990/1080;background:#0d0d0d;overflow:hidden}
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-v{
    position:absolute !important;inset:0 !important;
    width:100% !important;height:100% !important;
    object-fit:cover !important;display:block !important;
    transform-origin:center center !important;   /* zoom out of the middle, where the footage is */
    opacity:0;transition:opacity .28s ease;
  }
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-v.on{opacity:1}

  /* Same skin as pg-landing's .pgx-tharrow, and stamped just as hard for the
     same reason: the base theme paints every button as a black bar with pseudo
     overlays. Its own class, not .pgx-tharrow, because that one is display:none
     under 900px -- on a phone these arrows and the dots are the only manual way
     through the slideshow, so they stay visible at every width. */
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-arrow{
    position:absolute !important;top:50% !important;bottom:auto !important;transform:translateY(-50%) !important;
    z-index:5 !important;width:44px !important;height:44px !important;
    min-width:0 !important;min-height:0 !important;max-width:none !important;
    padding:0 0 4px !important;margin:0 !important;border:0 !important;border-radius:50% !important;
    background:#7A2FA2 !important;background-color:#7A2FA2 !important;background-image:none !important;
    color:#fff !important;font-size:26px !important;line-height:1 !important;font-weight:400 !important;
    font-family:'Poppins','Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif !important;
    display:flex !important;align-items:center !important;justify-content:center !important;
    text-transform:none !important;letter-spacing:0 !important;text-indent:0 !important;
    overflow:hidden !important;isolation:isolate !important;cursor:pointer !important;
    box-shadow:0 10px 24px -10px rgba(0,0,0,.75) !important;
    animation:none !important;transition:background .15s ease !important;
  }
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-arrow::before,
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-arrow::after{display:none !important;content:none !important;background:none !important;animation:none !important;opacity:0 !important;border:0 !important}
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-arrow:hover{background:#8B44BE !important;background-color:#8B44BE !important}
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-arrow.prev{left:10px !important;right:auto !important}
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-arrow.next{right:10px !important;left:auto !important}
  @media (max-width:600px){
    #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-arrow{width:38px !important;height:38px !important;font-size:22px !important}
  }

  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-dots{
    position:absolute;left:0;right:0;bottom:9px;z-index:5;
    display:flex;justify-content:center;align-items:center;gap:6px;flex-wrap:wrap;padding:0 12px;
  }
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-dot{
    width:7px !important;height:7px !important;min-width:0 !important;min-height:0 !important;
    padding:0 !important;margin:0 !important;border:0 !important;border-radius:50% !important;
    background:rgba(255,255,255,.42) !important;background-image:none !important;
    box-shadow:0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,.6) !important;cursor:pointer !important;
    animation:none !important;transition:background .15s ease,width .15s ease !important;
  }
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-dot::before,
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-dot::after{display:none !important;content:none !important}
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-dot.on{background:#fff !important;width:18px !important;border-radius:999px !important}

  /* NO mobile aspect override. pg-landing squared this slot off at 1:1, which
     is just another box too wide for the footage and showed the same bars. The
     one 990x1080 box and its zoom apply at every width. */
</style>
<script>
(function(){
  /* The hero is display:none for good, so stop it pulling down a full-size copy
     of a photo nobody sees. pg-landing holds its own reference to the element
     and may set a src back on it when a photo is clicked; that costs nothing
     while it is hidden. */
  var hero = document.getElementById('pgx-hero-img');
  if (hero) hero.removeAttribute('src');

  /* One clip per variant, in the product's own option order so the dots read
     left-to-right the same way the dropdown does. `v` must stay character-exact
     with the variant option value -- that string IS the key the buy-box selects
     and the cart's variant picker both report, and matching is what points the
     slideshow at the right clip. Renaming a variant in Shopify without editing
     this list leaves that one design falling back to slide 0. `z` is how far
     that clip has to be zoomed to push its baked-in pillarbox off the edges of
     the box; see the ASPECT note above before changing one.

     `r` is playback speed, per clip rather than one global rate: the nine newer
     ones run at 2.5x and the original at its own pace. It is applied in three
     places on purpose -- defaultPlaybackRate before the source is attached,
     because load() resets an element to its defaults and playbackRate is one of
     the things it clears; playbackRate at the swap, for browsers that do not
     carry the default onto a newly attached source; and once more from the play
     watchdog, the only code that runs after a retried play(). Setting it in any
     one of those alone is not enough. */
  var VIDS = [
    {z: 1, r: 1, v: "Legendary Poke Trio", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/372c232efb264da688df7ddecba254c7/372c232efb264da688df7ddecba254c7.HD-720p-4.5Mbps-92098881.mp4"},
    {z: 1.66, r: 2.5, v: "Blue Eyes, Dark Magician, Red Eyes", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/9d6a76bbc7504f589d013b52660475b2/9d6a76bbc7504f589d013b52660475b2.HD-720p-4.5Mbps-92296781.mp4"},
    {z: 1.66, r: 2.5, v: "Gengar Evolution", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/34da042ae6e64494be9fdfcbfc58b43d/34da042ae6e64494be9fdfcbfc58b43d.HD-720p-3.0Mbps-92296780.mp4"},
    {z: 1.66, r: 2.5, v: "Blastoise Evolution", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/d5d5246b3616411ca0ed05f1b982f44c/d5d5246b3616411ca0ed05f1b982f44c.HD-720p-3.0Mbps-92296779.mp4"},
    {z: 1.66, r: 2.5, v: "EX Legendary Poke Trio", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/8a2173f6b8ca40c2b5819534a0be89f9/8a2173f6b8ca40c2b5819534a0be89f9.HD-720p-4.5Mbps-92296778.mp4"},
    {z: 1.66, r: 2.5, v: "Eeveelutions", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/74b84084bd414500a949f60c972172b0/74b84084bd414500a949f60c972172b0.HD-720p-3.0Mbps-92296777.mp4"},
    {z: 1.66, r: 2.5, v: "Mewtwo EX, V, V Star", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/28bedf6bfa444dc68d3aa93170d0f0d8/28bedf6bfa444dc68d3aa93170d0f0d8.HD-720p-2.1Mbps-92296776.mp4"},
    {z: 1.66, r: 2.5, v: "One Piece", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/79e22ac3490d404f80990b5e764ec531/79e22ac3490d404f80990b5e764ec531.HD-720p-3.0Mbps-92296775.mp4"},
    {z: 1.66, r: 2.5, v: "Dragon Ball", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/8c2e1751c07744dd9846e44ad1050462/8c2e1751c07744dd9846e44ad1050462.HD-720p-3.0Mbps-92296774.mp4"},
    {z: 1.66, r: 2.5, v: "Goku", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/1a5d4284598e442da8c01b753ee09909/1a5d4284598e442da8c01b753ee09909.HD-720p-2.1Mbps-92296773.mp4"}
  ];

  function boot(){
    var box = document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-vid');
    if (!box || box.querySelector('.pgw-stage')) return false;

    var stage = document.createElement('div');
    stage.className = 'pgw-stage';

    function mk(){
      var v = document.createElement('video');
      v.className = 'pgw-v';
      v.muted = true; v.loop = true; v.playsInline = true;
      v.setAttribute('muted','');           /* iOS autoplays only with the attribute present */
      v.setAttribute('loop','');
      v.setAttribute('playsinline','');
      v.setAttribute('preload','none');     /* nothing is fetched until a slide is asked for */
      stage.appendChild(v);
      return v;
    }
    var a = mk(), b = mk(), front = a, cur = -1, want = -1, token = 0, inflight = null;

    var prev = document.createElement('button');
    prev.type = 'button'; prev.className = 'pgw-arrow prev';
    prev.setAttribute('aria-label','Previous video'); prev.innerHTML = '&#8249;';
    var next = document.createElement('button');
    next.type = 'button'; next.className = 'pgw-arrow next';
    next.setAttribute('aria-label','Next video'); next.innerHTML = '&#8250;';

    var dots = document.createElement('div');
    dots.className = 'pgw-dots';
    var dotEls = VIDS.map(function(item, i){
      var d = document.createElement('button');
      d.type = 'button'; d.className = 'pgw-dot';
      d.setAttribute('aria-label', item.v);
      d.addEventListener('click', function(){ show(i); });
      dots.appendChild(d);
      return d;
    });

    stage.appendChild(prev); stage.appendChild(next); stage.appendChild(dots);

    /* pg-landing's own <video> is replaced only now that the stage is built, so
       a failure above leaves the original single video untouched on screen. */
    box.innerHTML = '';
    box.appendChild(stage);

    function paintDots(){
      dotEls.forEach(function(d, i){ d.classList.toggle('on', i === want); });
    }

    /* THE SUPERVISOR -- a frozen slide fixes itself within a second.

       This replaced a burst of retries fired once per swap, which looked right
       and protected almost nothing: play() flips `paused` to false the moment it
       is called, long before a frame is on screen, so the retry loop saw
       "playing" on its first look and stopped. Anything that stopped playback
       AFTER that -- a rejected play promise, a stall while the opening clip was
       still hogging the connection, a decode hiccup, the tab being backgrounded
       -- left a still frame with nothing watching it. That matches the report
       exactly: the clip sat frozen, and paging away and back (a fresh load, by
       then cached) played it.

       So playback is judged on whether currentTime actually MOVES, not on the
       paused flag, and it is judged forever rather than for one second after a
       swap. Every clip loops, so a running slide always advances; a slide that
       has not advanced between two ticks is wrong no matter how it got there,
       and gets another play(). Calling play() on a video that is already playing
       is a no-op, so the common path costs nothing. */
    var lastT = -1, lastFront = null;
    setInterval(function(){
      var v = front;
      if (!v || !v.getAttribute('src')) return;
      if (v !== lastFront){ lastFront = v; lastT = -1; }   /* new slide: no history yet */

      var rate = (cur >= 0 && VIDS[cur]) ? VIDS[cur].r : 1;
      if (v.playbackRate !== rate) v.playbackRate = rate;

      var t = v.currentTime;
      var moved = (lastT >= 0) && (t > lastT + 0.01 || t < lastT);  /* t < lastT = looped */
      var stuck = v.paused || (lastT >= 0 && !moved && v.readyState >= 2);
      lastT = t;
      if (stuck){
        var p = v.play();
        if (p && p.catch) p.catch(function(){});
      }
    }, 700);

    /* Last resort: if the browser refused to autoplay this element outright, the
       first tap or key anywhere on the page is a user gesture and lifts that
       block. Costs nothing when playback is already fine. */
    function nudge(){
      var v = front;
      if (v && v.paused){ var p = v.play(); if (p && p.catch) p.catch(function(){}); }
    }
    ['pointerdown','touchstart','keydown'].forEach(function(ev){
      document.addEventListener(ev, nudge, true);
    });

    function show(i){
      if (i < 0 || i >= VIDS.length || i === want) return;
      want = i;                            /* the slide ASKED for, updated now */
      paintDots();

      /* WHICH ELEMENT THE CLIP LOADS INTO. Normally the one that is not on
         screen. But when a slide is ALREADY loading, that pending element is the
         off-screen one -- so this must reuse it rather than work out a fresh
         answer from `front`, which still points at the visible clip and would
         hand back the element the shopper is watching.

         That is the bug this replaced. Two show() calls overlapping (the init
         call still loading when a variant change or an arrow arrives) had the
         second one take `front` as its incoming element: it reset the src of the
         clip on screen, then added and immediately removed its own `on` class,
         leaving the visible slide invisible AND paused by the outgoing timer.
         Recovery needed a page past and back, which is exactly what was
         reported for the two slides nearest the start -- the ones reachable
         while the opening clip is still loading. */
      var incoming = inflight || ((front === a) ? b : a);
      if (incoming === front) return;
      inflight = incoming;

      var mine = ++token;                  /* a later request wins; this one goes quiet */
      var swapped = false;

      function swap(){
        if (swapped || mine !== token) return;
        swapped = true;
        inflight = null;

        /* read the outgoing element HERE, not when show() was called: a swap
           that happened in between would have moved it. */
        var outgoing = front;

        incoming.style.transform = 'scale(' + VIDS[i].z + ')';
        incoming.playbackRate = VIDS[i].r;
        incoming.classList.add('on');
        var p = incoming.play();
        if (p && p.catch) p.catch(function(){});

        if (outgoing !== incoming){
          outgoing.classList.remove('on');
          /* `outgoing !== front` and not a token check: the token moves on every
             request, which used to leave a superseded clip playing forever, and
             a stale timer could pause whatever had since become visible. Asking
             whether this element is still off screen is the question that
             actually matters. */
          setTimeout(function(){
            if (outgoing !== front) { try { outgoing.pause(); } catch(e){} }
          }, 320);
        }

        front = incoming; cur = i; paintDots();
      }

      /* drop the previous attempt's listener -- once:true only removes it if it
         actually fired, and a load that was superseded never fires */
      if (incoming.pgwOn) incoming.removeEventListener('loadeddata', incoming.pgwOn);
      incoming.pgwOn = swap;
      incoming.addEventListener('loadeddata', swap, {once: true});
      incoming.defaultPlaybackRate = VIDS[i].r;   /* what load() will restore to */
      incoming.src = VIDS[i].u;
      try { incoming.load(); } catch(e){}
      /* loadeddata does not fire on every browser and network combination -- swap
         anyway rather than leaving the shopper on the previous design's clip. */
      setTimeout(swap, 1800);
    }

    /* Stepped from `want`, not `cur`. `cur` only moves once a clip has loaded, so
       on a slow connection every click during the wait recomputed the SAME
       neighbour and restarted the same download -- measured in a throttled
       harness, six clicks advanced the slideshow by one slide and kept
       cancelling the load that would have ended the wait. Stepping from what was
       last requested makes each click count. */
    prev.addEventListener('click', function(){ show((want - 1 + VIDS.length) % VIDS.length); });
    next.addEventListener('click', function(){ show((want + 1) % VIDS.length); });

    function indexOf(s){
      if (!s) return -1;
      s = String(s).trim();
      for (var i = 0; i < VIDS.length; i++){ if (VIDS[i].v === s) return i; }
      return -1;
    }

    /* ANY select that reports a known design name moves the slideshow. Delegated
       from the document on purpose: this has to cover the buy-box dropdowns
       (pg-landing's [data-pgx-size] selects, which exist at load) AND the
       variant picker inside the cart drawer, which is rebuilt from scratch every
       time the cart changes -- a listener bound directly to that element would
       be thrown away with it. Capture phase, so it still fires if a handler
       downstream stops the event. A select on the page whose value happens to
       equal a variant name would also drive it; nothing else on this template
       carries those strings. */
    document.addEventListener('change', function(e){
      var t = e.target;
      if (!t || t.tagName !== 'SELECT') return;
      var i = indexOf(t.value);
      if (i < 0 && t.options && t.selectedIndex >= 0){
        var o = t.options[t.selectedIndex];
        if (o) i = indexOf(o.textContent);
      }
      if (i >= 0) show(i);
    }, true);

    /* open on whatever the buy box is already showing */
    var s1 = document.querySelector('#pgx [data-pgx-size="s1"]');
    var start = s1 ? indexOf(s1.value) : -1;
    show(start >= 0 ? start : 0);
    return true;
  }

  /* pg-landing renders .pgx-vid server-side, so it is normally there already;
     the retry only covers this section being evaluated before it. */
  if (!boot()){
    var tries = 0;
    var t = setInterval(function(){
      if (boot() || ++tries > 40) clearInterval(t);
    }, 150);
  }
})();
</script>

{% schema %}
{"name": "PG Wall Art gallery", "settings": [], "presets": [{"name": "PG Wall Art gallery"}]}
{% endschema %}
